#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048319 :topic/difficulty 1.336246886499386 :topic/id #uuid "9484137b-5aab-5e6f-af43-c4c224fcda06" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059665 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059496 :question/id #uuid "1d8679d3-8922-5471-811f-60c8c41c853a" :question/math-academy-id "e-3522" :question/problem "Express $\\cos\\left( x - \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059666 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059497 :question/id #uuid "bdb79801-7c8f-5b39-8730-85dcc3d9cdd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-3523" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\cos \\left( -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} \\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $$\n\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{\\pi}{12} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.\n$$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(x - y) = \\cos{x}\\cos{y} + \\sin{x}\\sin{y}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $y = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059628 :question/id #uuid "b643e820-52c3-5c08-a099-aecbed7b2b2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9991" :question/problem "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = 60^\\circ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :topic/math-academy-id 274 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050986}] :topic/title "The Sum and Difference Formulas for Cosine"}]}
